# Ribbons and areas: geom_ribbon

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_ribbon()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_ribbon.html), which covers
`geom_area()` and `stat_align()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows
ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code
and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

For each `x`, `geom_ribbon()` draws a band from `ymin` to `ymax`. `geom_area()` is the band from 0
up to `y`, and stacks groups on one another as stacked bars do, so it shows how the parts of a
whole vary along `x`. Its stat, `stat_align()`, interpolates the groups to a common set of `x`
values so that they stack. Giggly has `Giggly.Plot.geom_ribbon/3` and `Giggly.Plot.geom_area/3`,
which aligns its groups as `stat_align()` does and stacks them unless given `position: :identity`;
both take `orientation:` and `outline_type:`. It has no `stat_align()` to give another geom, and
no `stat` option to turn an area's alignment off. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn
with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so
the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell
with `Kino.Image`.

## A ribbon and an area

R's `LakeHuron` holds the level of Lake Huron, in feet, for each year from 1875 to 1972.

```r
# Generate data
huron <- data.frame(year = 1875:1972, level = as.vector(LakeHuron))
h <- ggplot(huron, aes(year))

h + geom_ribbon(aes(ymin=0, ymax=level))
h + geom_area(aes(y = level))
```

`LakeHuron` is a time series in base R rather than a table, so the cell writes out its 98 levels
as R gives them and builds `huron` from them, as R's code does. A number in a mapping is a
constant, as `ymin=0` is in R's.

```elixir
huron = %{
  "year" => Enum.to_list(1875..1972),
  "level" => [
    580.38, 581.86, 580.97, 580.8, 579.79, 580.39, 580.42, 580.82, 581.4, 581.32, 581.44, 581.68,
    581.17, 580.53, 580.01, 579.91, 579.14, 579.16, 579.55, 579.67, 578.44, 578.24, 579.1, 579.09,
    579.35, 578.82, 579.32, 579.01, 579.0, 579.8, 579.83, 579.72, 579.89, 580.01, 579.37, 578.69,
    578.19, 578.67, 579.55, 578.92, 578.09, 579.37, 580.13, 580.14, 579.51, 579.24, 578.66, 578.86,
    578.05, 577.79, 576.75, 576.75, 577.82, 578.64, 580.58, 579.48, 577.38, 576.9, 576.94, 576.24,
    576.84, 576.85, 576.9, 577.79, 578.18, 577.51, 577.23, 578.42, 579.61, 579.05, 579.26, 579.22,
    579.38, 579.1, 577.95, 578.12, 579.75, 580.85, 580.41, 579.96, 579.61, 578.76, 578.18, 577.21,
    577.13, 579.1, 578.25, 577.91, 576.89, 575.96, 576.8, 577.68, 578.38, 578.52, 579.74, 579.31,
    579.89, 579.96
  ]
}

h = Plot.new(huron, %{x: :year})

h
|> Plot.geom_ribbon(%{ymin: 0, ymax: :level})
|> render.()
```

```elixir
h
|> Plot.geom_area(%{y: :level})
|> render.()
```

## A horizontal area

```r
# Orientation cannot be deduced by mapping, so must be given explicitly for
# flipped orientation
h + geom_area(aes(x = level, y = year), orientation = "y")
```

```elixir
h
|> Plot.geom_area(%{x: :level, y: :year}, orientation: :y)
|> render.()
```

## A band around a line

```r
# Add aesthetic mappings
h +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = level - 1, ymax = level + 1), fill = "grey70") +
  geom_line(aes(y = level))
```

`level - 1` is written as a capture of the function that computes it, and the band's fill, R's
`"grey70"`, as its hex, `"#B3B3B3"`, since CSS has no such name.

```elixir
h
|> Plot.geom_ribbon(
  %{ymin: {&Kernel.-/2, [:level, 1]}, ymax: {&Kernel.+/2, [:level, 1]}},
  fill: "#B3B3B3"
)
|> Plot.geom_line(%{y: :level})
|> render.()
```

## Groups at different x values

```r
# The underlying stat_align() takes care of unaligned data points
df <- data.frame(
  g = c("a", "a", "a", "b", "b", "b"),
  x = c(1, 3, 5, 2, 4, 6),
  y = c(2, 5, 1, 3, 6, 7)
)
a <- ggplot(df, aes(x, y, fill = g)) +
  geom_area()

# Two groups have points on different X values.
a + geom_point(size = 8) + facet_grid(g ~ .)
```

`facet_grid(g ~ .)` gives each group a row of its own: `g` left of the `~` names the rows'
column, and `.` right of it names none for the columns, so `Giggly.Plot.facet_grid/2` takes it as
`rows: :g`.

```elixir
df = %{
  "g" => ["a", "a", "a", "b", "b", "b"],
  "x" => [1, 3, 5, 2, 4, 6],
  "y" => [2, 5, 1, 3, 6, 7]
}

a =
  df
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :x, y: :y, fill: :g})
  |> Plot.geom_area()

a
|> Plot.geom_point(size: 8)
|> Plot.facet_grid(rows: :g)
|> render.()
```

## Aligned points

```r
# stat_align() interpolates and aligns the value so that the areas can stack
# properly.
a + geom_point(stat = "align", position = "stack", size = 8)
```

Points drawn with the areas' stat and position show the values `stat_align()` computes, stacked.

Not in Giggly yet: `stat_align()` given to a geom as `stat = "align"`, and `geom_point()`'s
`position` option, here `position = "stack"`.

## Areas without alignment

```r
# To turn off the alignment, the stat can be set to "identity"
ggplot(df, aes(x, y, fill = g)) +
  geom_area(stat = "identity")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_area()`'s `stat` option, here `stat = "identity"`.